{"name":"Aged Eucalyptus","hex":"#898253","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aged","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Old.","Having the age of."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Old people, collectively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aged"},{"term":"eucalyptus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many trees, of genus Eucalyptus, native mainly to Australia.","Any tree of the tribe Eucalypteae, especially of the genera Corymbia and Angophora formerly classified in the Eucalyptus genus."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eucalyptus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eucalyptus","blocks":[{"text":"Eucalyptus is a genus of more than 700 species of flowering plants in the family Myrtaceae. Most species of Eucalyptus are trees, often mallees, and a few are shrubs. Along with several other genera in the tribe Eucalypteae, including Corymbia and Angophora, they are commonly known as eucalypts or \"gum trees\". Species of Eucalyptus have bark that is either smooth, fibrous, hard, or stringy and leaves that have oil glands. The sepals and petals are fused to form a \"cap\" or operculum over the stamens, hence the name from Greek eû 'well' and kaluptós 'covered'."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Eucalyptus_tereticornis_flowers%2C_capsules%2C_buds_and_foliage.jpeg/330px-Eucalyptus_tereticornis_flowers%2C_capsules%2C_buds_and_foliage.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eucalyptus","wikidata":"Q45669"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Eucalyptus_tereticornis_flowers%2C_capsules%2C_buds_and_foliage.jpeg/500px-Eucalyptus_tereticornis_flowers%2C_capsules%2C_buds_and_foliage.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Eucalyptus_tereticornis_flowers%2C_capsules